	abstract = {Data and security integrity, increasingly severe challenges for the logistics industry, are affected by the further development of digital transformation as well as escalating complexity in global supply chains. Emerging cyber threats, particularly with the advent of quantum computing, render current encryption techniques insecure, necessitating the development of new methods to protect logistics networks. This chapter explores how quantum computing can enhance data integrity and security in logistics. Key quantum concepts, such as superposition, entanglement, and quantum parallelism, are explored and discussed in this study regarding their applications in logistics to enhance encryption, validate data, and facilitate secure communication. The idea of using post-quantum algorithms, such as hash-based and lattice-based techniques, to protect logistics data against quantum attacks is examined through the analysis of quantum-resistant cryptography. Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) was introduced as a technique for generating unbreakable quantum keys for confidential data exchange among supply chain actors. Another research trend was investigated through the use of quantum blockchain for transparent and tamper-proof supply chain delivery. Quantum technologies could be applied in practice and solve integration challenges in logistics; some case studies may also provide future directions. Therefore, although quantum computing promises significant advancements, issues such as cost, integration, and expertise need to be addressed for the prospective adoption of quantum computing to become widespread. The combination of innovative security solutions with quantum computing would enable logistics to future-proof their operations through robust data integrity, building a much more resilient global supply chain network. Β© 2026 Nova Science Publishers, Inc.},
