	abstract = {Linkable ring signatures (LRS) allow a signer to anonymously sign a message on behalf of a set of users (the ring), while enabling public detection when multiple signatures are produced by the same signer. These primitives support privacy-preserving applications such as e-cash, e-voting, and decentralized ledgers. We present a Multivariate Linkable Ring Signature (MQ-LRS) scheme built on the hardness of the multivariate quadratic (MQ) problem and a strengthened zero-knowledge identification protocol. The scheme achieves existential unforgeability against chosen message attack (EUF-CMA) in the random-oracle model (ROM), honest-verifier zero-knowledge (HVZK), special soundness, anonymity, and non-slanderability. We further extend the design to support separable linkability, threshold-extendable signing, and event-oriented linkability without weakening its security guarantees. The strengthened soundness enables reducing the repetition count required for Ξ» -bit soundness from roughly 1.71 Ξ» to Ξ» , significantly lowering signature size and computation. The scheme provides a signature size that is approximately 12.76, 2.77, and 1.78 times smaller than code-based, lattice-based, and other MQ-based linkable ring signature schemes, respectively, at the 128-bit security level. The proposed MQ-LRS offers a quantum-resistant, feature-rich solution suitable for privacy-sensitive applications that require compact keys and flexible linking semantics. Β© 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
