	abstract = {The advent of fault-tolerant quantum computers poses an existential threat to the current blockchain technology, which relies on cryptographic primitives like elliptic-curve cryptography and SHA-256 hashing. This manuscript surveys the emerging field of quantum-secure blockchains, categorizing the main research directions into two paradigms. The first, post-quantum blockchain, seeks to replace classical cryptographic elements with quantum-resistant algorithms. The second, more radical approach aims to construct an intrinsically quantum blockchain, where the ledgerβ€™s security and state are encoded directly in quantum mechanical principles. We delve into three promising intrinsic schemes: those based on Greenbergerβ€“Horneβ€“Zeilinger (GHZ) states and entanglement in time, those leveraging multi-time states and pseudo-density matrices, and hypergraph-based approaches. As the principal original contribution of this work, we present a comprehensive theoretical framework for a topological quantum blockchain based on non-Abelian anyons, providing the first detailed encoding scheme mapping classical blockchain data to braiding sequences. We further develop the connection to Chernβ€“Simons theory, establishing a field-theoretic foundation where the blockchainβ€™s history is encoded in Wilson loops, and its immutability follows from topological and gauge invariance. Extending this framework, we introduce a holographic AdS/CFT interpretation, revealing that the topological blockchain can be understood as a dual description of a black hole analog in anti-de Sitter space, where the blockchainβ€™s history is encoded in the microstates of a black hole and linking braids between blocks correspond to wormholes. We provide a detailed physical and mathematical analysis of each scheme, comparing their security assumptions, resource requirements, and feasibility in the near and long terms. The topological approach, in particular, offers a compelling new path toward a blockchain with inherent fault tolerance, where the chainβ€™s history is encoded in the topology of anyon worldlines, making it naturally resistant to decoherence and local tampering. Β© 2026 by the author.},
	author_keywords = {AdS/CFT; braid groups; Chernβ€“Simons theory; GHZ states; Jones polynomial; non-Abelian anyons; post-quantum cryptography; quantum blockchain; quantum entanglement; topological quantum computing},
	keywords = {Blockchain; Computation theory; Fault tolerance; Fault tolerant computer systems; Geometry; Gravitation; Group theory; Public key cryptography; Quantum computers; Quantum cryptography; Topology; AdS/CFT; Block-chain; Braid groups; Chern-Simons theory; Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger state; Jones polynomial; Non-Abelian anyons; Post quantum cryptography; Quantum blockchain; Quantum Computing; Topological quantum computing; Quantum entanglement},
