	abstract = {Cloud applications require strong security and privacy controls, especially when users and services operate across different regions and platforms. The existing block-chain-based Identity Management, Access Control and Secure Sharing (BC-IAS) helps to implement access policies using blockchain and cryptographic techniques. However, BC-IAS depends on hyperelliptic curve cryptography (HECC), which is not secure against quantum attacks, and uses centralized identity management, which limits scalability and flexibility in global cloud environments. To address this, a Post-Quantum Federated Identity Management Privacy Compliance Enforcement System (PQ-FIM-PCES), which uses postquantum cryptography, such as CRYSTALS-Kyber for encryption and CRYSTALS-Dilithium for digital signatures, is proposed. Federated Identity Management (FIM) allows users to authenticate across multiple domains using verifiable credentials without depending on a single identity provider. The proposed PQ-FIM-PCES uses blockchain smart contracts to verify identities, enforce access rules, and maintain secure audit trials. Overall, the proposed method includes three stages: scheduling federated identities, securing data with postquantum signcryption, and validating access through a block chain. In testing, PQ-FIM-PCES achieved an authentication success rate of 99.36 %, which is better than that of the existing model and suitable for privacy compliance in current cloud environments. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
