	abstract = {As first described by Satoshi Nakamoto in his 2008 Bitcoin whitepaper, blockchain technology has emerged from a decentralized currency system to foundational technology to spur innovation across industries. The three main pillars of blockchain technologyβ€”decentralization, cryptographic security through concepts such as elliptic curve cryptography and Merkle trees, and immutabilityβ€”help to mitigate the trust issues inherent in centralized systems. At a technical level, blockchain networks leverage consensus algorithms like energy-intensive Proof of Work (PoW) and energy-saving Proof of Stake (PoS) protocols, as well as layered arrangements that incorporate base-layer protocols (Layer 1) and scaling solutions such as rollups and state, payment, or channel networks (Layer 2). Advanced cryptography, particularly zero-knowledge proofs (zk-SNARKs, zk-STARKs), facilitates the optimization of privacy coupled with verification. Empirical facts are witnessing revolutionary adoption (financial networks tokenized 30% of all transaction volumes by 2024; supply chains used blockchain-based passports to verify ethical sourcing). Ethereum phased its energy use down by 99.95% by transitioning to PoS, and Layer 2 solutions improved throughput by 10β€“100x. However, the blockchain trilemma of security, scalability, and decentralization, remains visible, and sharding and hybrid consensus models are suggested solutions. This progress represents the potential for blockchain to reshape digital trust, although regulatory diversity and quantum attacks create tensions that necessitate constant progression in post-quantum cryptography and cross-chain interoperability. Β© The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
