	abstract = {The dynamic growth of Internet of Things (IoT) has allowed data monitoring and automation in a wide variety of areas, consequently, caused severe consequences in data security and integrity vulnerability. This study suggests the usage of a Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) blockchain framework that will protect IoT communications against classical and quantum computing attacks. The framework incorporates Kyber Key Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM) to provide lightweight, quantum-resistant confidentiality and Dilithium digital signatures to provide strong authentication along with the SHA3-256 hashing algorithm to assure data integrity. A permissioned blockchain with Proofof-Authority (PoA) consensus makes it efficient to validate transactions and in an immutable format. Python 3.13 using PQClean libraries was experimentally evaluated and consisted of simulating IoT devices broadcasting environmental telemetry to the nodes of the blockchain. The dataset consisted of the payloads of different size of 64 bytes up to 1024 bytes, and both normal and adversarial network environments. Tests show encryption/decryption times of less than 1.5 ms, a signature verification time of less than 1.5 ms, transaction commit time of fewer than 75 ms and integrity verification accuracy better than 90 percent even with attacks. The suggested solution comprises a scalable security layer of the IoT systems, which ensures the confidentiality, authenticity, and integrity of the sent data and is low latency and quantum secure, preserving the efficiency of the work of the operational solution. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
