	abstract = {With the advent of quantum computing, traditional cryptographic systems based on classical hard problems (e.g., RSA, ECC) face significant vulnerabilities against quantum algorithms such as Shor's algorithm. Supersingular elliptic curve isogenies have emerged as a promising alternative for postquantum cryptography. This paper presents a comprehensive exploration of isogeny-based protocols in blockchain environments, focusing on enhanced security for transactions and improved performance of consensus mechanisms. We propose new cryptographic schemes founded on isogenies, accompanied by formal definitions, theorems, rigorous proofs, and detailed mathematical demonstrations. We further provide an in-depth performance and security analysis supported by case studies and simulations, and we discuss the practical implications and future directions of this approach.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
