	affiliations = {College of Computer Sciences and Technology, Qingdao University, Qingdao, 266071, China; School of Computing and Communications, Lancaster University, Lancaster, LA1 4WA, United Kingdom; School of Electronics Engineering and Computer Science, Peking University, Beijing, 100871, China; State Key Laboratory of Cryptology, P.O. Box 5159, Beijing, 100878, China},
	abstract = {Password-based authenticated key exchange ( PAKEPAKE) protocol, a widely used authentication mechanism to realize secure communication, allows protocol participants to establish a high-entropy session key by pre-sharing a low-entropy password. An open challenge in PAKEPAKE is how to design a quantum-resistant round-optimal PAKEPAKE. To solve this challenge, lattice-based cryptography is a promising candidate for post-quantum cryptography. In addition, Katz and Vaikuntanathan (ASIACRYPT'09) design the first three-round PAKEPAKE protocol by leveraging the smooth projective hash function ( SPHFSPHF) over lattices. Subsequently, Zhang and Yu (AISACRYPT'17) optimized Katz-Vaikuntanathan's approximate SPHFSPHF via a splittable public key encryption. They then constructed a two-round PAKEPAKE by using the simulation-sound non-interactive zero-knowledge (NIZK) proofs, but how to construct a lattice-based simulation-sound NIZK remains an open research question. In other words, how to design a one-round PAKEPAKE via an efficient lattice-based SPHFSPHF still remains a challenge. In this work, we attempt to fill this gap by proposing a lattice-based SPHFSPHF with adaptive smoothness. We then obtain a one-round PAKEPAKE protocol over lattices with rigorous security analysis by integrating the proposed SPHFSPHF into the one-round framework proposed by Katz and Vaikuntananthan (TCC'11). Furthermore, we explore the possibilities of achieving two-round PAKEPAKE and universal composable (UC) security from our SPHFSPHF, and show the potential application of our PAKEPAKE in Internet of Things (IoTs) where communication cost is the main consideration.  Β© 2008-2012 IEEE.},
