	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85097850800?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {IBM Research, ZΓΌrich, Switzerland; Aix Marseille University, CNRS, Centrale Marseille, I2M, Marseille, France; DGA, Paris, France; University of Birmingham, Birmingham, United Kingdom; Univ. Bordeaux, CNRS, Bordeaux INP, IMB, UMR 5251, Talence, F-33400, France; INRIA, IMB, UMR 5251, Talence, F-33400, France; LIX, CNRS, Ecole Polytechnique, Institut Polytechnique de Paris, Paris, France; INRIA, Rocquencourt, France; UniversitΓ© libre de Bruxelles, Brussels, Belgium},
	abstract = {We introduce a new signature scheme, SQISign, (for Short Quaternion and Isogeny Signature) from isogeny graphs of supersingular elliptic curves. The signature scheme is derived from a new one-round, high soundness, interactive identification protocol. Targeting the post-quantum NIST-1 level of security, our implementation results in signatures of 204 bytes, secret keys of 16 bytes and public keys of 64 bytes. In particular, the signature and public key sizes combined are an order of magnitude smaller than all other post-quantum signature schemes. On a modern workstation, our implementation in C takes 0.6Β s for key generation, 2.5Β s for signing, and 50Β ms for verification. While the soundness of the identification protocol follows from classical assumptions, the zero-knowledge property relies on the second main contribution of this paper. We introduce a new algorithm to find an isogeny path connecting two given supersingular elliptic curves of known endomorphism rings. A previous algorithm to solve this problem, due to Kohel, Lauter, Petit and Tignol, systematically reveals paths from the input curves to a β€specialβ€™ curve. This leakage would break the zero-knowledge property of the protocol. Our algorithm does not directly reveal such a path, and subject to a new computational assumption, we prove that the resulting identification protocol is zero-knowledge. Β© 2020, International Association for Cryptologic Research.},
