	volume = {11898 LNCS},
	pages = {3 - 22},
	doi = {10.1007/978-3-030-35423-7_1},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85077114157?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {imec-COSIC, KU Leuven, Leuven, Belgium; Inria and Sorbonne Universities/UPMC Uni Paris 6, Paris, France; Versailles Laboratory of Mathematics, UVSQ, Versailles, France; Orange, Meylan, France; Versailles Laboratory of Mathematics, UVSQ, CNRS, University of Paris-Saclay, Versailles, France},
	abstract = {In this document, we introduce (Formula Presented): a Digital Signature Scheme based on the Permuted Kernel Problem (PKP) [23]. PKP is a simple NP-hard [10] combinatorial problem that consists of finding a kernel for a publicly known matrix, such that the kernel vector is a permutation of a publicly known vector. This problem was used to develop an Identification Scheme (IDS) which has a very efficient implementation on low-cost smart cards. From this zero-knowledge identification scheme, we derive (Formula Presented) with the traditional Fiat-Shamir transform [9]. Thus, (Formula Presented) has a security that can be provably reduced, in the (classical) random oracle model, to the hardness of random instances of PKP (or, if wanted, to any specific family of (Formula Presented) instances). We propose parameter sets following the thorough analysis of the State-of-the-art attacks on PKP presented in [17]. We show that (Formula Presented) is competitive with other signatures derived from Zero-Knowledge identification schemes. In particular, PKP-DSS-128 gives a signature size of approximately 20 KBytes for 128 bits of classical security, which is approximately (Formula Presented) smaller than MQDSS. Moreover, our proof-of-concept implementation shows that PKP-DSS-128 is an order of magnitude faster than MQDSS which in its turn is faster than Picnic2, SPHINCS,.. Since the (Formula Presented) is NP-hard and since there are no known quantum attacks for solving PKP significantly better than classical attacks, we believe that our scheme is post-quantum secure. Β© 2019, Springer Nature Switzerland AG.},
